Case Study:

2
00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:02.827
Distributed Cache.

3
00:00:03.107 --> 00:00:07.246
Chapter 44, from the Case Studies part of the System Design Playbook.

4
00:00:08.996 --> 00:00:09.956
At a glance:

5
00:00:10.236 --> 00:00:11.409
Chapter at a glance.

6
00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:12.692
Reader bands:

7
00:00:12.972 --> 00:00:15.255
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

8
00:00:15.535 --> 00:00:24.921
Ch 07 (using caches: patterns, invalidation, stampedes), Ch 10, Ch 11 (consistent hashing), What you will build:

9
00:00:25.201 --> 00:00:34.503
The cache system itself, the platform's shared, Redis-compatible cache tier used by product cards, sessions, rate limits, feature stores and presence.

10
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:48.735
This is the design Memcached or Redis interview question: the server's data structures and eviction, its threading and protocol, partitioning and online resharding, replication and failover, and running it as a platform.

11
00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:54.881
Ch 07 was about using a cache; this chapter is about building and operating one.

12
00:00:56.231 --> 00:00:58.066
L1: eviction policies.

13
00:00:58.346 --> 00:01:10.037
Four policies (exact LRU, Redis-style sampled LRU, decayed LFU, W-TinyLFU) ran over 2M requests on Zipf, scan-polluted and shifting traces.

14
00:01:10.317 --> 00:01:21.453
W-TinyLFU beat LRU by 9.1 points at 10k slots (48.6% versus 39.5%) and resisted scans.

15
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:31.823
After an abrupt popularity shift, frequency history lost to LRU at larger sizes (66.3% versus 68.8%).

16
00:01:32.103 --> 00:01:36.711
Sampled LRU stayed within 0.3 points of exact LRU.

17
00:01:38.061 --> 00:01:41.219
L2: build a cache server, benchmark it like Redis.

18
00:01:41.499 --> 00:01:47.515
A ~150-line RESP server with virtual threads, driven by the stock redis-benchmark.

19
00:01:47.795 --> 00:01:56.541
It served ~50k ops per second without pipelining and ~260 to 410k with 16-deep pipelining.

20
00:01:56.821 --> 00:02:01.515
The global lock showed only once pipelining removed the round-trip bottleneck.

21
00:02:01.795 --> 00:02:07.747
Real Redis did ~2x better (0.6 to 0.84M pipelined).

22
00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:13.403
It stored a key in 152 bytes; the JVM cache needed 212.

23
00:02:14.753 --> 00:02:17.697
L3: live resharding of a real Redis Cluster.

24
00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:20.963
A 4th master was added and rebalanced under load.

25
00:02:21.243 --> 00:02:24.273
25% of keys moved, all to the new node.

26
00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:27.539
Modulo hashing would have moved 75%.

27
00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:35.457
A client that follows MOVED or ASK redirects had 0 errors (3,126 redirects followed).

28
00:02:35.737 --> 00:02:40.302
A client with a stale slot map had 11,706 errors.

29
00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:44.276
L4: what failover loses (real Redis).

30
00:02:44.556 --> 00:02:49.292
The replica stalled for 300 ms and the primary was killed during the stall.

31
00:02:49.572 --> 00:02:56.505
Asynchronous replication lost thousands of acknowledged writes (6,000 to 15,000 across runs).

32
00:02:56.785 --> 00:03:08.007
With WAIT 1 100, 0 acknowledged writes were lost: the writes made during the stall were never confirmed, at up to ~200 ms extra latency for those batches.

33
00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.268
Reading time:

34
00:03:09.548 --> 00:03:11.340
110 min + code.

35
00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.543
Part 1.

36
00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:15.743
Introduction and Why It Matters.

37
00:03:16.993 --> 00:03:19.511
A story: the cache that became a database.

38
00:03:19.791 --> 00:03:23.673
The grocery platform's Redis started as a cache for product cards.

39
00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:36.135
Over two years it also became: the session store, the cart, rate-limit counters (Ch 39), the feature store (Ch 43), presence (Ch 41) and a few temporary queues.

40
00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:37.119
Then:

41
00:03:37.399 --> 00:03:39.319
Memory ran out at 7 pm.

42
00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:46.489
The eviction policy was allkeys-lru, so Redis evicted carts and sessions to make room for product cards.

43
00:03:46.769 --> 00:03:48.860
Customers were logged out mid-checkout.

44
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.705
A catalogue export read every product once at midnight and flushed the hot set.

45
00:03:53.985 --> 00:03:56.887
The database took the full morning load at 6 am.

46
00:03:58.237 --> 00:03:59.879
Scaling out broke clients.

47
00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:05.514
Adding shards to the client-side modulo-hash ring remapped 75% of keys.

48
00:04:05.794 --> 00:04:09.463
The cache emptied, and so did the patience of the database team.

49
00:04:09.743 --> 00:04:11.322
A failover lost carts.

50
00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:16.295
The primary died; the replica, a few hundred milliseconds behind, was promoted.

51
00:04:16.575 --> 00:04:19.007
Items customers had just added vanished.

52
00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:20.717
One key melted a node.

53
00:04:20.997 --> 00:04:25.775
A flash sale's product card was read 80k times a second from one shard.

54
00:04:27.125 --> 00:04:39.136
The rebuild separated workloads into clusters by what they are allowed to lose, chose eviction per cluster, moved to hash-slot clustering with cluster-aware clients, and wrote down durability rules.

55
00:04:40.486 --> 00:04:41.169
Quote:

56
00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:43.113
The thesis of this chapter.

57
00:04:43.393 --> 00:04:54.294
A distributed cache is a memory-bound, partitioned, replicated key-value server, and every design decision trades hit ratio, latency, capacity and durability.

58
00:04:54.574 --> 00:04:57.433
Eviction decides hit ratio per byte (L1).

59
00:04:57.713 --> 00:05:02.705
Threading and protocol decide throughput, and round trips dominate everything (L2).

60
00:05:02.985 --> 00:05:10.494
Partitioning decides how painfully you scale: hash slots move only what must move, if clients follow redirects (L3).

61
00:05:10.774 --> 00:05:17.387
Replication is asynchronous by default, so a cache is allowed to lose data, design so that it can (L4).

62
00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:22.425
And the most important decision is organisational: what is allowed in the cache at all.

63
00:05:23.775 --> 00:05:26.079
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

64
