94
00:06:23.466 --> 00:06:24.789
Learning objectives.

95
00:06:25.069 --> 00:06:26.967
By the end you will be able to:

96
00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:33.071
Design the cache server: data structures, memory accounting, eviction and expiry (L1).

97
00:06:33.351 --> 00:06:39.751
Explain server threading and protocol performance: event loops, pipelining, I or O threads (L2).

98
00:06:40.031 --> 00:06:44.127
Partition and reshard online with hash slots and redirects (L3).

99
00:06:44.407 --> 00:06:48.525
Replicate and fail over, and state exactly what can be lost (L4).

100
00:06:49.875 --> 00:06:56.445
Operate the cache as a platform: multi-tenancy, hot keys, big keys, observability and capacity.

101
00:06:57.795 --> 00:06:58.670
Part 2.

102
00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.827
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

103
00:07:02.077 --> 00:07:04.339
Intuition: a warehouse's front shelf.

104
00:07:04.619 --> 00:07:08.331
The warehouse (database) has everything but is slow to fetch from.

105
00:07:08.611 --> 00:07:10.915
The front shelf (cache) is small and fast.

106
00:07:11.195 --> 00:07:26.576
The questions: what to put on the shelf and what to remove when it's full (eviction); how many clerks serve customers and how (threading); how to split the shelf across several counters (partitioning); what happens when a counter closes (replication and failover).

107
00:07:26.856 --> 00:07:30.483
And one rule: nothing on the front shelf should be the only copy.

108
00:07:31.833 --> 00:07:32.793
Requirements.

109
00:07:33.073 --> 00:07:33.926
Functional.

110
00:07:34.206 --> 00:07:44.318
GET or SET or DEL with TTLs; data structures (hashes, sorted sets, counters); atomic operations and scripts; pub or sub for invalidation.

111
00:07:44.598 --> 00:07:49.675
Horizontal partitioning; online resharding; replicas and automatic failover.

112
00:07:49.955 --> 00:07:52.793
Eviction policies per cluster; memory limits.

113
00:07:53.073 --> 00:07:54.182
Non-functional.

114
00:07:54.462 --> 00:07:59.987
Sub-millisecond p99 server latency; millions of ops per second per cluster.

115
00:08:00.267 --> 00:08:04.854
Availability 99.95%+; failover in seconds.

116
00:08:06.204 --> 00:08:09.233
Predictable memory: no surprise OOM kills.

117
00:08:09.513 --> 00:08:12.052
Multi-tenant isolation between teams.

118
00:08:13.402 --> 00:08:14.447
Estimation.

119
00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:16.071
Here is a quick table:

120
00:08:16.351 --> 00:08:17.781
Quantity, and Estimate.

121
00:08:18.061 --> 00:08:31.458
Peak ops: ~1.5M ops per second across clusters (product cards ~600k, features ~500k, rate limits ~300k, sessions ~100k).

122
00:08:31.738 --> 00:08:43.322
Per node: ~150 to 250k ops per second for simple commands per Redis process on modern cores without pipelining; far more with pipelining (L2).

123
00:08:43.602 --> 00:08:44.391
Memory:

124
00:08:44.671 --> 00:09:05.109
Product cards 2M times ~2 KB about 4 GB; features 10M keys times ~100 B about 1 GB; sessions 5M times ~1 KB about 5 GB; plus overhead (L2: ~90 B per key before the value) and fragmentation (~1.2x).

125
00:09:05.389 --> 00:09:06.797
Nodes: catalog:

126
00:09:07.077 --> 00:09:10.554
6 masters + replicas (throughput-bound); sessions:

127
00:09:10.834 --> 00:09:13.501
3 masters + replicas (memory-bound).

128
00:09:14.851 --> 00:09:18.029
The server: data structures, memory and expiry.

129
00:09:18.309 --> 00:09:27.205
Main dictionary: a hash table with incremental rehashing (Redis moves a few buckets per operation, so resizing never pauses the server).

130
00:09:27.485 --> 00:09:33.608
Compact encodings: small hashes, lists and sets stored as packed arrays (listpack) until they grow.

131
00:09:33.888 --> 00:09:37.109
That's why many small hashes can be cheaper than many keys.

132
00:09:37.389 --> 00:09:43.427
Memory accounting: every key costs overhead (dictionary entry, key object, value object).

133
00:09:43.707 --> 00:09:55.632
L2 measured 152 B per key with 64-byte values in Redis, and 212 B in a naive JVM implementation (object headers, boxing).

134
00:09:55.912 --> 00:10:01.565
With jemalloc, fragmentation adds 10 to 30%; active defragmentation helps.

135
00:10:02.915 --> 00:10:13.198
Expiry: lazy (checked on access) plus active (a periodic job samples keys with TTLs and deletes expired ones, repeating while many are found).

136
00:10:13.478 --> 00:10:16.614
Neither needs a global sorted list of expiry times.

137
00:10:16.894 --> 00:10:25.961
Big keys: a 50 MB hash blocks the single thread when deleted or serialised; use UNLINK (background free) and cap value sizes.

138
00:10:27.311 --> 00:10:28.143
Eviction.

139
00:10:28.423 --> 00:10:31.175
When memory hits maxmemory, something must go.

