241
00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:14.670
For keys already moved it answers ASK, try the target, once, with ASKING.

242
00:15:14.950 --> 00:15:20.411
After the slot's ownership changes, requests get MOVED, telling clients to update their map.

243
00:15:20.691 --> 00:15:31.934
The smart client in L3 followed 3,126 redirects with 0 errors; a client with a stale map failed 11,706 reads.

244
00:15:33.284 --> 00:15:41.732
Hash tags (sku123) force related keys into the same slot, so multi-key commands and Lua scripts work in a cluster.

245
00:15:42.012 --> 00:15:48.433
Clients must be cluster-aware (Lettuce, Jedis, redis-py cluster), or sit behind a proxy that is.

246
00:15:49.783 --> 00:15:51.490
Replication and failover.

247
00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:59.002
Asynchronous replication: the primary acknowledges writes before replicas receive them, streaming a replication log.

248
00:15:59.282 --> 00:16:03.741
Replicas that fall behind resync partially (backlog) or fully (snapshot).

249
00:16:04.021 --> 00:16:04.938
Failover:

250
00:16:05.218 --> 00:16:12.279
Sentinel (for non-cluster setups) or cluster nodes detect a failed primary by timeout and promote a replica.

251
00:16:12.559 --> 00:16:15.354
The promoted replica may lack the latest writes.

252
00:16:15.634 --> 00:16:20.861
L4, with a 300 ms replica stall, lost thousands of acknowledged writes.

253
00:16:22.211 --> 00:16:26.413
WAIT n timeout makes the client wait until n replicas have the write.

254
00:16:26.693 --> 00:16:33.563
In L4 it lost 0 acknowledged writes, because writes during the stall were never confirmed, and the client knew.

255
00:16:33.843 --> 00:16:42.824
That's synchronous-ish replication at the cost of latency, but not consensus: a promotion can still pick a replica without the write when several exist.

256
00:16:43.104 --> 00:16:49.397
Split brain: a partitioned old primary may keep accepting writes until min-replicas-to-write stops it.

257
00:16:49.677 --> 00:16:51.747
Those writes are lost when it rejoins.

258
00:16:52.027 --> 00:16:53.072
Persistence:

259
00:16:53.352 --> 00:17:01.629
RDB snapshots and AOF (append-only file, fsync every second) protect against restarts, not against failover gaps.

260
00:17:02.979 --> 00:17:05.369
The honest rule: a cache may lose data.

261
00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:14.481
Anything that must not be lost lives in a database, with the cache as a rebuildable copy (write-through or write-behind with a durable source).

262
00:17:14.761 --> 00:17:18.601
Stronger guarantees need a consensus-based store (Ch 12).

263
00:17:19.951 --> 00:17:21.487
Hot keys and big keys.

264
00:17:21.767 --> 00:17:41.009
Hot keys concentrate on one slot and node: client-side near-caches (Ch 38 L3), read replicas with replica reads, key replication (card:X1..8 read at random, Ch 07), and server-side client tracking (Redis 6 invalidation-based client caching).

265
00:17:41.289 --> 00:17:46.409
Detection: redis-cli --hotkeys (with LFU), sampling proxies, per-slot metrics.

266
00:17:46.689 --> 00:17:53.281
Big keys: split large collections, cap sizes, use UNLINK, find them with --bigkeys or MEMORY USAGE.

267
00:17:54.631 --> 00:17:56.594
Multi-tenancy and governance.

268
00:17:56.874 --> 00:18:02.783
Separate clusters by loss tolerance and eviction policy (§1.2), not by team.

269
00:18:03.063 --> 00:18:09.101
A shared cluster with allkeys-lru will evict someone's session to cache someone else's product card.

270
00:18:09.381 --> 00:18:17.807
Key naming conventions with owner prefixes, mandatory TTLs on cache clusters, and per-tenant memory budgets watched in dashboards.

271
00:18:18.087 --> 00:18:23.549
ACLs per service (Redis 6 ACL users, command restrictions, key patterns).

272
00:18:23.829 --> 00:18:27.349
Change control on dangerous commands and configuration.

273
00:18:28.699 --> 00:18:29.872
Operating the tier.

274
00:18:30.152 --> 00:18:42.355
Metrics: ops per second, latency (server and client), hit ratio per key prefix, evictions, expirations, memory and fragmentation, replication lag, connected clients, slow log.

275
00:18:42.635 --> 00:18:53.515
Capacity: plan for memory at peak plus headroom for fork-based snapshots (copy-on-write can need up to 2x memory during BGSAVE under heavy writes) and resharding.

276
00:18:53.795 --> 00:18:56.781
Upgrades: rolling, replicas first, then failover.

277
00:18:58.131 --> 00:18:59.987
Managed versus self-hosted:

278
00:19:00.267 --> 00:19:10.870
ElastiCache or MemoryDB or Azure Cache handle failover and patching; MemoryDB adds a durable transaction log for primary-grade durability.

279
00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.351
Interview framing.

280
00:19:13.631 --> 00:19:32.831
Start from requirements and loss tolerance, then: the server's hash table and memory; eviction (LRU versus LFU versus admission) with numbers; threading and pipelining; partitioning with hash slots and live migration (MOVED or ASK); replication, failover and what is lost; hot keys.

281
00:19:33.111 --> 00:19:42.305
Staff-level signals: separating clusters by durability, noeviction for non-rebuildable data, and saying clearly that a cache is not a database.

