00:22:58.279 --> 00:23:04.935
R1 p99 1 ms server-side, 3 ms client-side for single-key operations.

335
00:23:05.215 --> 00:23:09.332
R2 No loss of carts or sessions on any single-node failure.

336
00:23:10.682 --> 00:23:15.695
R3 Scale out by one shard without a hit-ratio drop of more than 5 points.

337
00:23:15.975 --> 00:23:20.391
R4 Catalogue hit ratio ≥ 90% at peak.

338
00:23:20.671 --> 00:23:22.762
R5 No cross-tenant evictions.

339
00:23:23.042 --> 00:23:24.450
Decisions in detail.

340
00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:26.863
Clusters (ADR-300).

341
00:23:27.143 --> 00:23:30.322
Separate endpoints, separate memory budgets and alerts.

342
00:23:30.602 --> 00:23:32.949
Eviction (ADR-301).

343
00:23:33.229 --> 00:23:41.613
LFU for catalog with lfu-decay-time 1; session cluster alerts at 70% memory, pages at 85%.

344
00:23:42.963 --> 00:23:46.312
Clients (ADR-302 or 303).

345
00:23:46.592 --> 00:23:53.504
Lettuce cluster client, topology refresh on MOVED and every 60 s; pipelining via async API.

346
00:23:53.784 --> 00:23:56.344
Durability (ADR-304).

347
00:23:56.624 --> 00:23:57.413
Carts:

348
00:23:57.693 --> 00:24:06.056
PostgreSQL is the source of truth; cache updated after commit; WAIT for session writes; AOF everysec on the session cluster.

349
00:24:06.336 --> 00:24:08.832
Hot keys (ADR-305).

350
00:24:09.112 --> 00:24:15.107
1 to 5 s near-cache for read-mostly hot keys; replicate flash-sale keys times 8.

351
00:24:15.387 --> 00:24:16.411
Consequences.

352
00:24:16.691 --> 00:24:23.709
More clusters to run; client configuration standards to enforce; slightly higher write latency on sessions.

353
00:24:23.989 --> 00:24:29.963
In return: no cross-tenant evictions, painless scale-out, and clear rules about what may be lost.

