127.0.0.1:7003 7,500 keys ██████████████████.

450
00:35:00.703 --> 00:35:02.218
The rest is in the chapter.

451
00:35:05.068 --> 00:35:05.921
Reading it:

452
00:35:06.201 --> 00:35:16.847
Movement: exactly the keys in the slots handed to the new node moved (7,527, 25%), and nothing moved between the old nodes.

453
00:35:17.127 --> 00:35:18.727
The key counts end up even.

454
00:35:19.007 --> 00:35:28.116
Modulo comparison: hash % 3, then hash % 4 would have moved 22,496 keys (75%).

455
00:35:28.396 --> 00:35:31.425
For a cache, that's a mass miss storm onto the database.

456
00:35:32.775 --> 00:35:40.477
Clients: the smart client followed 3,126 MOVED and 1 ASK redirect and never failed.

457
00:35:40.757 --> 00:35:47.690
The static client failed 11,706 reads, one for every read of a key whose slot had moved.

458
00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:51.191
No reads were misses: the data travels with the slots.

459
00:35:52.541 --> 00:35:54.419
L4, What failover loses.

460
00:35:54.699 --> 00:35:55.637
Requirement:

461
00:35:55.917 --> 00:36:00.525
Know exactly which acknowledged writes can disappear when the cache primary dies.

462
00:36:00.805 --> 00:36:02.597
A real primary and replica.

463
00:36:02.877 --> 00:36:09.491
The client writes pipelined batches while the replica is stalled with SIGSTOP and the primary is killed with SIGKILL.

464
00:36:09.771 --> 00:36:15.531
Then the replica is resumed, promoted, and checked against every write the client believed was done.

465
00:36:16.881 --> 00:36:20.017
Here is the Java code, FailoverL4.java.

466
00:36:20.297 --> 00:36:24.542
It runs to 131 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

467
00:36:24.822 --> 00:36:35.510
FailoverL4.java, run with: java FailoverL4.java (JDK 21; redis-server 7 on the PATH; Linux, uses kill -STOP/-KILL).

468
00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:37.326
WHAT A CACHE FAILOVER LOSES.

469
00:36:37.606 --> 00:36:47.377
Real Redis: a primary (:6391) and a replica (:6392) with the default ASYNCHRONOUS. replication.

470
00:36:47.657 --> 00:36:54.014
A client writes session or cart keys in pipelined batches of 50 and records every write the primary.

471
00:36:56.864 --> 00:36:57.781
On screen:

472
00:36:58.061 --> 00:37:05.357
Primary:6391, then replica:6392 (async replication).

473
00:37:05.637 --> 00:37:16.304
Replica stalls 300 ms, primary is. killed during the stall. write mode writes sent acknowledged lost (acked batch p50 batch max. but. missing).

474
00:37:16.584 --> 00:37:30.109
ASYNC (reply OK = done) 327,150 327,100 6,000 0.16 ms 25.02 ms.

475
00:37:30.389 --> 00:37:52.341
WAIT 1 100 after each batch 170,800 170,650 0 0.23 ms 201.06 ms. then asynchronous replication acknowledges a write before any replica has it, so a. failover during replica lag. silently discards acknowledged writes.

476
00:37:52.621 --> 00:37:56.440
WAIT makes the client find out: batches. written during the stall.

477
00:37:56.720 --> 00:37:58.235
The rest is in the chapter.

478
00:38:01.085 --> 00:38:01.938
Reading it:

479
00:38:02.218 --> 00:38:07.039
ASYNC: the primary acknowledged writes during the replica's 300 ms stall.

480
00:38:07.319 --> 00:38:09.410
When it died, those writes died with it.

481
00:38:09.690 --> 00:38:15.151
The run above lost 6,000 acknowledged writes; other runs lost up to 15,000.

482
00:38:15.431 --> 00:38:24.839
WAIT 1 100: batches written during the stall got 0 replica confirmations within 100 ms, so the client never counted them as done.

483
00:38:25.119 --> 00:38:27.125
0 acknowledged writes were lost.

484
00:38:27.405 --> 00:38:34.551
The cost: those batches waited ~100 to 200 ms, and every batch pays one extra round trip.

485
00:38:35.901 --> 00:38:37.693
The lesson isn't always WAIT.

486
00:38:37.973 --> 00:38:44.949
It's that asynchronous replication makes a cache a lossy store, so anything that must survive lives in the database.

487
00:38:46.299 --> 00:38:47.238
Part 6.

488
00:38:47.518 --> 00:38:48.798
Production Concerns.

489
00:38:50.048 --> 00:38:51.200
Failure modes.

490
00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:52.824
Here is a quick table:

491
00:38:53.104 --> 00:38:55.387
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

492
00:38:55.667 --> 00:38:57.139
Wrong eviction policy.

493
00:38:57.419 --> 00:38:58.229
Symptom:

494
00:38:58.509 --> 00:39:00.856
Hot set flushed by scans (L1).

495
00:39:01.136 --> 00:39:02.139
Mitigation:

496
00:39:02.419 --> 00:39:04.936
LFU or admission for catalogue data.

497
00:39:05.216 --> 00:39:06.987
Shared cluster, mixed data.

498
00:39:07.267 --> 00:39:08.077
Symptom:

499
00:39:08.357 --> 00:39:10.533
Sessions evicted for product cards.

500
00:39:10.813 --> 00:39:11.816
Mitigation:

501
00:39:12.096 --> 00:39:14.400
Clusters by loss tolerance, noeviction.

502
00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:16.173
Round-trip-heavy clients.

503
00:39:16.453 --> 00:39:17.264
Symptom:

504
00:39:17.544 --> 00:39:19.293
Latency blow-up (L2).

505
00:39:19.573 --> 00:39:20.576
Mitigation:

506
00:39:20.856 --> 00:39:22.627
Pipelining, MGET, scripts.

507
00:39:22.907 --> 00:39:24.123
Modulo sharding.

508
00:39:24.403 --> 00:39:25.213
Symptom:

509
00:39:25.493 --> 00:39:27.541
Mass remap on scale-out (L3).

510
00:39:27.821 --> 00:39:28.824
Mitigation:

511
00:39:29.104 --> 00:39:31.045
Hash slots or consistent hashing.

512
00:39:31.325 --> 00:39:32.904
Stale client topology.

513
00:39:33.184 --> 00:39:33.995
Symptom:

514
00:39:34.275 --> 00:39:36.344
Errors during resharding (L3).

515
00:39:36.624 --> 00:39:37.627
Mitigation:

516
00:39:37.907 --> 00:39:40.893
Cluster-aware clients following MOVED or ASK.

517
00:39:41.173 --> 00:39:42.944
Async replication failover.

518
00:39:43.224 --> 00:39:44.035
Symptom:

519
00:39:44.315 --> 00:39:46.491
Lost acknowledged writes (L4).

520
00:39:46.771 --> 00:39:47.773
Mitigation:

521
00:39:48.053 --> 00:39:51.168
Source of truth elsewhere, WAIT, min-replicas-to-write.

522
00:39:52.518 --> 00:39:53.457
Hot key.

523
00:39:53.737 --> 00:39:54.547
Symptom:

524
00:39:54.827 --> 00:39:56.299
One node saturated.

525
00:39:56.579 --> 00:39:57.582
Mitigation:

526
00:39:57.862 --> 00:40:00.635
Near-cache, key replication, replica reads.

527
00:40:00.915 --> 00:40:02.473
Big key or slow command.

528
00:40:02.753 --> 00:40:03.563
Symptom:

529
00:40:03.843 --> 00:40:05.486
Server-wide latency spike.

530
00:40:05.766 --> 00:40:06.769
Mitigation:

531
00:40:07.049 --> 00:40:09.609
Limits, UNLINK, SCAN, renamed commands.

532
00:40:09.889 --> 00:40:11.339
Fork during snapshot.

533
00:40:11.619 --> 00:40:12.430
Symptom:

534
00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:14.033
Memory spike, OOM.

535
00:40:14.313 --> 00:40:15.315
Mitigation:

536
00:40:15.595 --> 00:40:17.707
Headroom, snapshots on replicas.

537
00:40:19.057 --> 00:40:20.145
Observability.

538
00:40:20.425 --> 00:40:26.143
Hit ratio per key prefix (client-side metrics), evictions and expirations per second.

539
00:40:26.423 --> 00:40:30.241
Latency histograms (LATENCY, client timers), slow log.

540
00:40:30.521 --> 00:40:34.916
Memory used versus maxmemory, fragmentation ratio, big keys.

541
00:40:35.196 --> 00:40:40.529
Replication lag and link status; failover events; cluster state and open slots.

542
00:40:40.809 --> 00:40:43.220
Top keys per shard (hot-key detection).

543
00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:45.509
Security.

544
00:40:45.789 --> 00:40:54.002
No cache exposed to the internet; TLS in transit; ACL users per service with key patterns and command restrictions.

545
00:40:54.282 --> 00:40:58.527
Disable or rename FLUSHALL, KEYS, CONFIG for application users.

546
00:40:58.807 --> 00:41:03.757
Don't store secrets or unencrypted personal data you wouldn't store in the database.

547
00:41:05.107 --> 00:41:06.003
Performance.

548
00:41:06.283 --> 00:41:11.957
Pipelining and batching first (L2); then key design (small hashes, compact encodings).

549
00:41:12.237 --> 00:41:19.213
I or O threads for high connection counts; connection pooling or multiplexing clients (Lettuce multiplexes).

550
00:41:19.493 --> 00:41:22.928
Keep values small; compress large payloads client-side.

551
00:41:24.278 --> 00:41:25.003
Cost.

552
00:41:25.283 --> 00:41:32.537
Memory is the cost driver: compact encodings, TTLs on everything cacheable, and not caching what isn't read.

553
00:41:32.817 --> 00:41:40.753
Right-size by hit ratio per GB: the marginal gigabyte buys little on a flattened hit curve (Ch 38 L3).

554
00:41:41.033 --> 00:41:45.406
Managed services trade a premium for operations (Ch 35).

555
00:41:46.756 --> 00:41:47.695
Part 7.

556
00:41:47.975 --> 00:41:49.681
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

557
00:41:50.931 --> 00:41:51.849
Watch out:

558
00:41:52.129 --> 00:41:52.769
1.

559
00:41:53.049 --> 00:41:54.841
The cache as the only copy.

560
00:41:55.121 --> 00:41:57.915
Failover loses acknowledged writes (L4).

561
00:41:59.265 --> 00:42:00.183
Watch out:

562
00:42:00.463 --> 00:42:01.167
2.

563
00:42:01.447 --> 00:42:03.025
One cluster for everything.

564
00:42:03.305 --> 00:42:06.356
Eviction policies and loss tolerance differ by data.

565
00:42:07.706 --> 00:42:08.623
Watch out:

566
00:42:08.903 --> 00:42:09.650
3.

567
00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:11.850
LRU for scan-heavy workloads.

568
00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:14.647
One export flushes the hot set (L1).

569
00:42:15.997 --> 00:42:16.915
Watch out:

570
00:42:17.195 --> 00:42:17.899
4.

571
00:42:18.179 --> 00:42:19.693
One command per round trip.

572
00:42:19.973 --> 00:42:22.960
The network, not the server, is the bottleneck (L2).

573
00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:25.227
Watch out:

574
00:42:25.507 --> 00:42:26.275
5.

575
00:42:26.555 --> 00:42:27.771
Modulo sharding.

576
00:42:28.051 --> 00:42:30.398
Scale-out remaps most keys (L3:

577
00:42:30.678 --> 00:42:32.086
75%).

578
00:42:33.436 --> 00:42:34.353
Watch out:

579
00:42:34.633 --> 00:42:35.380
6.

580
00:42:35.660 --> 00:42:37.089
Cluster-unaware clients.

581
00:42:37.369 --> 00:42:39.844
Every resharding becomes errors (L3).

582
00:42:41.194 --> 00:42:42.111
Watch out:

583
00:42:42.391 --> 00:42:43.138
7.

584
00:42:43.418 --> 00:42:44.613
KEYS in production.

585
00:42:44.893 --> 00:42:46.215
Blocks the single thread.

586
00:42:47.565 --> 00:42:48.483
Watch out:

587
00:42:48.763 --> 00:42:49.445
8.

588
00:42:49.725 --> 00:42:50.664
Big keys.

589
00:42:50.944 --> 00:42:53.845
Deleting or serialising them stalls the server.

590
00:42:55.195 --> 00:42:56.113
Watch out:

591
00:42:56.393 --> 00:42:57.139
9.

592
00:42:57.419 --> 00:42:58.507
No TTLs.

593
00:42:58.787 --> 00:43:01.177
Memory fills with data nobody reads.

594
00:43:02.527 --> 00:43:03.444
Watch out:

595
00:43:03.724 --> 00:43:05.815
10. allkeys- eviction on sessions.

596
00:43:06.095 --> 00:43:08.399
Users logged out under memory pressure.

597
00:43:09.749 --> 00:43:10.666
Watch out:

598
00:43:10.946 --> 00:43:11.778
11.

599
00:43:12.058 --> 00:43:13.935
No memory headroom for forks.

600
00:43:14.215 --> 00:43:15.858
Snapshots trigger OOM.

601
00:43:17.208 --> 00:43:18.125
Watch out:

602
00:43:18.405 --> 00:43:19.195
12.

603
00:43:19.475 --> 00:43:20.797
Ignoring hot keys.

604
00:43:21.077 --> 00:43:23.083
Sharding doesn't spread one key's load.

605
00:43:24.433 --> 00:43:25.307
Part 8.

606
00:43:25.587 --> 00:43:26.931
Interview Q and A.

607
00:43:28.181 --> 00:43:29.013
Beginner.

608
00:43:29.293 --> 00:43:30.317
Question 1.

609
00:43:30.597 --> 00:43:33.371
Design a distributed cache, the main components?

610
00:43:33.651 --> 00:43:35.016
Thirty-second answer:

611
00:43:35.296 --> 00:43:56.181
Cache servers holding a hash table in memory with TTLs and an eviction policy; a partitioning scheme (hash slots or consistent hashing) so keys spread across nodes; replicas per shard with automatic failover; cluster-aware clients or a proxy for routing; and monitoring of hit ratio, latency, memory and replication lag.

612
00:43:57.531 --> 00:43:58.534
Question 2.

613
00:43:58.814 --> 00:44:00.243
LRU or LFU?

614
00:44:00.523 --> 00:44:01.889
Thirty-second answer:

615
00:44:02.169 --> 00:44:07.865
LRU adapts quickly to changing popularity but is fooled by scans and one-hit wonders.

616
00:44:08.145 --> 00:44:13.734
LFU keeps frequently used keys but lags when popularity shifts unless counts decay.

617
00:44:14.014 --> 00:44:24.233
Admission-based policies like W-TinyLFU combine a recency window with frequency-based admission and usually win, especially for small caches.

618
00:44:25.583 --> 00:44:26.628
Question 3.

619
00:44:26.908 --> 00:44:29.873
How do TTLs expire without scanning everything?

620
00:44:30.153 --> 00:44:31.519
Thirty-second answer:

621
00:44:31.799 --> 00:44:43.724
Lazily, check expiry when a key is accessed, plus actively: a periodic task samples keys with TTLs, deletes the expired ones and repeats while a high fraction is expired.

622
00:44:44.004 --> 00:44:51.385
No global ordered structure is needed, and memory from rarely accessed expired keys is reclaimed probabilistically.

623
00:44:52.735 --> 00:44:53.802
Question 4.

624
00:44:54.082 --> 00:44:57.005
Why is Redis single-threaded, and is that a problem?

625
00:44:57.285 --> 00:44:58.650
Thirty-second answer:

626
00:44:58.930 --> 00:45:07.783
Command execution on one thread avoids locks and makes each command atomic; the bottleneck is usually network round trips, not CPU.

627
00:45:08.063 --> 00:45:19.455
It's a problem for slow commands (which block everyone) and for using many cores in one process, solved by running more shards, I or O threads, or multi-threaded alternatives.

628
00:45:20.805 --> 00:45:21.808
Intermediate.

629
00:45:22.088 --> 00:45:23.197
Question 5.

630
00:45:23.477 --> 00:45:26.848
How do you partition a cache, and what happens when you add a node?

631
00:45:27.128 --> 00:45:28.493
Thirty-second answer:

632
00:45:28.773 --> 00:45:31.781
Avoid modulo hashing, which remaps most keys.

633
00:45:32.061 --> 00:45:39.357
Use consistent hashing with virtual nodes ( about 1/(N+1) of keys move) or hash slots (Redis Cluster:

634
00:45:39.637 --> 00:45:45.803
16,384 slots mapped to nodes; you move only chosen slots, gradually).

635
00:45:46.083 --> 00:45:50.200
Clients must route by the current map and follow redirects during moves.

636
00:45:51.550 --> 00:45:52.659
Question 6.

637
00:45:52.939 --> 00:45:54.518
What are MOVED and ASK?

638
00:45:54.798 --> 00:45:56.163
Thirty-second answer:

639
00:45:56.443 --> 00:45:57.958
Redis Cluster redirects.

640
00:45:58.238 --> 00:46:03.422
MOVED means the slot now lives on another node: update your slot map and retry there.

641
00:46:03.702 --> 00:46:12.747
ASK means the slot is being migrated and this key is already on the target: retry once on the target with ASKING, but don't update the map.

642
00:46:13.027 --> 00:46:15.801
Cluster-aware clients handle both transparently.

643
00:46:17.151 --> 00:46:18.239
Question 7.

644
00:46:18.519 --> 00:46:21.697
How do you make cache operations fast from the client side?

645
00:46:21.977 --> 00:46:23.343
Thirty-second answer:

646
00:46:23.623 --> 00:46:38.065
Minimise round trips: pipelining, MGET or HMGET, Lua scripts for read-modify-write; multiplexed connections; keep values small; co-locate clients with the cache (same AZ); near-cache hot keys in process.

647
00:46:39.415 --> 00:46:40.439
Question 8.

648
00:46:40.719 --> 00:46:42.746
How does replication work in Redis?

649
00:46:43.026 --> 00:46:44.391
Thirty-second answer:

650
00:46:44.671 --> 00:46:51.882
Asynchronous: the primary streams its command log to replicas, acknowledging clients before replicas apply writes.

651
00:46:52.162 --> 00:46:58.989
New or far-behind replicas get a snapshot plus the backlog; short disconnections resume from the backlog.

652
00:46:59.269 --> 00:47:01.957
Replicas can serve reads (possibly stale).

653
00:47:03.307 --> 00:47:04.373
Question 9.

654
00:47:04.653 --> 00:47:06.509
What eviction policy for sessions?

655
00:47:06.789 --> 00:47:08.155
Thirty-second answer:

656
00:47:08.435 --> 00:47:21.875
None, noeviction, with memory alerts well before the limit, a dedicated cluster, TTLs for idle sessions, and ideally a durable source (database) so a failover or restart doesn't log everyone out.

657
00:47:22.155 --> 00:47:24.523
Eviction is for data that can be rebuilt.

658
00:47:25.873 --> 00:47:26.641
Senior.

659
00:47:26.921 --> 00:47:27.966
Question 10.

660
00:47:28.246 --> 00:47:31.681
What can you lose when a cache primary fails, and how do you limit it?

661
00:47:31.961 --> 00:47:33.326
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:47:33.606 --> 00:47:42.907
With asynchronous replication, any write acknowledged but not yet replicated to the promoted replica, plus writes accepted by a partitioned old primary.

663
00:47:43.187 --> 00:47:57.822
Limit it with WAIT (clients know which writes are replicated), min-replicas-to-write (primary stops accepting writes without replicas), AOF for restarts, and, most importantly, keeping the truth in a database so the cache can be rebuilt.

664
00:47:59.172 --> 00:48:00.303
Question 11.

665
00:48:00.583 --> 00:48:03.484
How do you handle a hot key that saturates one shard?

666
00:48:03.764 --> 00:48:05.129
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:48:05.409 --> 00:48:19.404
Detect it (hotkeys, per-slot metrics), then: near-cache in each client for a second or two; replicate the key under N suffixes and read one at random; allow replica reads for it; or use server-assisted client caching.

668
00:48:19.684 --> 00:48:21.391
Pre-warm known campaign keys.

669
00:48:22.741 --> 00:48:23.829
Question 12.

670
00:48:24.109 --> 00:48:26.434
How would you reshard a live cluster safely?

671
00:48:26.714 --> 00:48:28.079
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:48:28.359 --> 00:48:48.669
Add the new node, move slots incrementally (a few at a time) with key migration, relying on ASK during and MOVED after; verify all clients are cluster-aware first; throttle migration to protect latency; watch hit ratio and latency; avoid big keys that block migration; do it off-peak with a rollback plan.

673
00:48:50.019 --> 00:48:51.299
Question 13.

674
00:48:51.579 --> 00:48:53.691
How do you estimate memory for a cache?

675
00:48:53.971 --> 00:48:55.336
Thirty-second answer:

676
00:48:55.616 --> 00:49:07.221
Keys times (key size + value size + per-key overhead), times a fragmentation factor, plus replication buffers, client buffers, and headroom for snapshots (copy-on-write).

677
00:49:07.501 --> 00:49:10.253
Measure overhead empirically for your encodings.

678
00:49:11.603 --> 00:49:12.798
Question 14.

679
00:49:13.078 --> 00:49:15.894
How do you protect a shared cache from one bad tenant?

680
00:49:16.174 --> 00:49:17.539
Thirty-second answer:

681
00:49:17.819 --> 00:49:32.582
Separate clusters for different loss tolerances; within a cluster, key prefixes per owner, ACLs restricting commands and key patterns, memory budgets with alerts, mandatory TTLs, slow-command limits, and client-side rate limits.

682
00:49:33.932 --> 00:49:34.828
Architect.

683
00:49:35.108 --> 00:49:36.303
Question 15.

684
00:49:36.583 --> 00:49:40.487
Design the caching platform for a national grocery app end to end.

685
00:49:40.767 --> 00:49:42.132
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:49:42.412 --> 00:50:02.828
Four clusters by loss tolerance (catalog LFU, sessions noeviction + durable source, rate limits volatile-ttl, features LRU); Redis Cluster with replicas and automatic failover; cluster-aware clients with pipelining; near-caches for hot keys; carts and sessions backed by PostgreSQL; ACLs,

687
00:50:03.108 --> 00:50:11.983
TTLs and budgets; dashboards for hit ratio, latency, memory and lag; resharding and failover runbooks tested in game days.

688
00:50:13.333 --> 00:50:14.527
Question 16.

689
00:50:14.807 --> 00:50:18.391
Redis versus Memcached versus an in-process cache, when each?

690
00:50:18.671 --> 00:50:20.037
Thirty-second answer:

691
00:50:20.317 --> 00:50:29.938
In-process (Caffeine) for the hottest, read-mostly data with short TTLs, no network hop, but per-pod duplication and invalidation fan-out.

692
00:50:30.218 --> 00:50:34.229
Memcached for simple, huge, multi-threaded key-value caching.

693
00:50:34.509 --> 00:50:40.183
Redis when you need data structures, atomic scripts, pub or sub, replication and clustering.

694
00:50:40.463 --> 00:50:41.999
Often all three in layers.

695
00:50:43.349 --> 00:50:44.693
Question 17.

696
00:50:44.973 --> 00:50:47.917
When should a cache become a primary datastore, if ever?

697
00:50:48.197 --> 00:50:49.563
Thirty-second answer:

698
00:50:49.843 --> 00:51:05.309
Only with a durable, consensus- or log-backed engine designed for it (for example, MemoryDB's multi-AZ transaction log, or a real database with an in-memory engine), clear durability SLOs, backups and recovery tests.

699
00:51:05.589 --> 00:51:10.901
Plain Redis with async replication should hold only rebuildable or loss-tolerant data.

700
00:51:12.251 --> 00:51:13.425
Question 18.

701
00:51:13.705 --> 00:51:15.667
How do you do cache capacity planning?

702
00:51:15.947 --> 00:51:17.313
Thirty-second answer:

703
00:51:17.593 --> 00:51:32.611
Measure the hit-ratio curve against memory from real traces (simulate with key samples), choose the knee, add overhead and headroom, and size by the larger of memory and throughput needs; revisit with traffic growth and eviction metrics.

704
00:51:33.961 --> 00:51:35.199
Question 19.

705
00:51:35.479 --> 00:51:37.676
How do you run caches across regions?

706
00:51:37.956 --> 00:51:39.321
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:51:39.601 --> 00:51:50.524
Keep caches regional and rebuildable from regional or replicated sources; invalidate via events that cross regions; avoid synchronous cross-region cache replication.

708
00:51:50.804 --> 00:51:58.953
For global data, active-active databases or CRDT-based caches (Redis Enterprise) exist, but add complexity.

709
00:52:00.303 --> 00:52:01.413
Question 20.

710
00:52:01.693 --> 00:52:04.295
What would you monitor and alert on for the cache tier?

711
00:52:04.575 --> 00:52:05.941
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:52:06.221 --> 00:52:16.119
Page on: latency SLO burn, memory above thresholds on noeviction clusters, failover events, replication link down, cluster state not OK.

713
00:52:16.399 --> 00:52:24.314
Ticket on: hit-ratio drops per prefix, eviction spikes, fragmentation, big keys, slow log entries, connection count growth.

714
00:52:25.664 --> 00:52:26.560
Part 9.

715
00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:28.248
Summary and Practice.

716
00:52:29.498 --> 00:52:30.586
Key takeaways.

717
00:52:30.866 --> 00:52:35.495
Separate clusters by what they may lose; noeviction for non-rebuildable data.

718
00:52:35.775 --> 00:52:41.599
Eviction matters: admission + frequency for skew and scans; recency for shifts (L1).

719
00:52:41.879 --> 00:52:46.210
Round trips dominate throughput and latency; pipeline and batch (L2).

720
00:52:46.490 --> 00:52:50.927
Hash slots + cluster-aware clients make scale-out routine (L3).

721
00:52:51.207 --> 00:52:56.647
Async replication loses acknowledged writes on failover; keep truth elsewhere (L4).

722
00:52:57.997 --> 00:53:00.984
Hot keys and big keys need specific handling.

723
00:53:02.334 --> 00:53:03.657
Decision cheat sheet.

724
00:53:03.937 --> 00:53:05.281
Here is a quick table:

725
00:53:05.561 --> 00:53:06.734
Need, and Choice.

726
00:53:07.014 --> 00:53:13.499
Catalogue-like cache: allkeys-lfu (or W-TinyLFU in process), TTL + jitter.

727
00:53:13.779 --> 00:53:15.081
Sessions or carts:

728
00:53:15.361 --> 00:53:19.179
Database truth, noeviction cluster, WAIT for replicated writes.

729
00:53:19.459 --> 00:53:20.419
Partitioning:

730
00:53:20.699 --> 00:53:24.091
Redis Cluster hash slots, hash tags for related keys.

731
00:53:24.371 --> 00:53:25.225
Clients:

732
00:53:25.505 --> 00:53:28.406
Cluster-aware, pipelining, topology refresh.

733
00:53:28.686 --> 00:53:29.625
Hot keys:

734
00:53:29.905 --> 00:53:32.678
Near-cache, key replication, replica reads.

735
00:53:32.958 --> 00:53:33.875
Failover:

736
00:53:34.155 --> 00:53:37.718
Replicas + automatic promotion; min-replicas-to-write.

737
